#: {{synonyms|en|glutition<q:obsolete>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Bulwer|J[ohn] B[ulwer]]]|chapter=Lip Gallantry, or Certaine Labiall Fashions Invented by Divers Nations|title=Anthropometamorphosis: Man Transform’d: Or, The Artificiall Changling Historically Presented,{{nb...|in the Mad and Cruell Gallantry, Foolish Bravery, Ridiculous Beauty, Filthy Finenesse, and Loathsome Loveliness of Most Nations, Fashioning and Altering Their Bodies from the Mould Intended by Natvre; with Figures of Those Transformations. To which Artificiall and Affected Deformations are Added, all the Native and Nationall Monstrosities that have Appeared to Disfigure the Humane Fabrick. With a Vindication of the Regular Beauty and Honesty of Nature. And an Appendix of the Pedigree of the English Gallant.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed by William Hunt|year=1653|page=189|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b30324257_0001/page/189/mode/1up|oclc=896193742|passage=[T]he action of the gullet, that is '''deglutition''', is performed and conſummated, the action being animall and partly Naturall.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Herman Boerhaave|[Herman] Boerhaave]]|chapter=Of the Origin, Nature, and Mixture of the ''Saliva'' with the Aliments|title=Dr. Boerhaave’s Academical Lectures on the Theory of Physic.{{nb...|Being a Genuine Translation of His Institutews and Explanatory Comment, Collated and Adjusted to Each Other, as They were Dictated to His Students at the University of Leyden.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for W. Innys,{{nb...|at the West End of St. Paul’s.}}|year=1742|volume=I (Containing the History of Physic,{{nb...|and the Oeconomy of the Several Parts Subservient to Chylification}})|section=§&nbsp;70|page=170|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=eVATq4FUq1gC&pg=PA170|oclc=938339039|passage=The ''Uvula'' {{...}} ſerves as an arched and ſlippery Bridge, exactly fitted every way to cover the convex Sides and ''Rima'' of the ''Glottis'', for the Aliment to ſlide eaſily along in '''Deglutition'''; it prevents the Aliment from falling out of the ''Fauces'' into the ''Larynx'' in '''Deglutition'''; and being moved by its Muſcles, protruds the Aliment backward into the upper Part of the ''Fauces''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Anthony Storck [''i.e.'', {{w|Anton von Störck}}]|chapter=Case VIII|title=An Essay on the Medicinal Nature of Hemlock. In Two Parts.{{nb...|To which is Annexed, a Necessary Supplement on the Subject. Translated from the Latin of Dr Anthony Storck, Aulic Counsellor, and One of the Principal Physicians to the Empress-Queen; and Physician to the City Pazmarian Hospital of Vienna.}}|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed for A. Donaldson and J. Reid, for [[w:Alexander Donaldson (bookseller)|Alex[ander] Donaldson]]|year=1762|section=part II|pages=108–109|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pGBZAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA109|oclc=723087741|passage=A man of the age of twenty-ſeven had a tubercle at the root of his tongue, for about ſix years; {{...}} '''Deglutitions''' were rendered extremely difficult.}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|volume=II|chapter=VI|page=174|passage=Feeling without judgment is a washy draught indeed; but judgment untempered by feeling is too bitter and husky a morsel for human '''deglutition'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Horace Bushnell|chapter=Of the Animal Infestations|title=Moral Uses of Dark Things|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles Scribner's Sons|Charles Scribner & Company]]|year=1868|pages=287–288|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=e_JMPxC9j8gC&pg=PA288|oclc=4647088|passage=Sometimes they work by satire, as in the case of the ants here referred to; sometimes by terror, by spitefulness, by cunning stealthiness and tricks of decoy, by immense '''deglutitions''', by any and all sorts of animal habits that connect, with prey, ferocities, voracities and disgusts that make it symbolic of evil.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:William Henry Hudson|W[illiam] H[enry] Hudson]]|chapter=The Village and ‘The Stones’|title=Afoot in England|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hutchinson (publisher)|Hutchinson & Co.]]{{nb...|Paternoster Row}}|year=1909|page=251|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/afootinengland00hudsgoog#page/n265/mode/1up|oclc=31584816|passage=Some philosopher has said that the chief pleasure in a man's life, as in that of a cow, consists in the processes of mastication, '''deglutition''', and digestion, and I am very much inclined to agree with him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gerard J. Tortora; Bryan Derrickson|chapter=The Digestive System|title=Principles of Anatomy and Physiology|edition=12th|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|Wiley]]|year=2008|page=935|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uNwfOPPYgKAC&pg=PA935|column=2|isbn=978-0-470-08471-7|passage=The movement of food from the mouth into the stomach is achieved by the act of swallowing, or '''deglutition'''{{nb...}}. '''Deglutition''' is facilitated by the secretion of saliva and mucus and involves the mouth, pharynx, and esophagus.}}

